16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 4–31, 1864","place":"Virginia, Washington, D.C., and New York City","result":"Early Northern confidence in Grant declined when Lee’s army remained intact and casualty reports increased; Confederate opinion remained confident even as Grant continued moving south","facts":[{"label":"Prisoners taken at the Mule Shoe","value":"More than 3,000"},{"label":"Cannon taken at the Mule Shoe","value":"20"},{"label":"Correspondents per major New York paper","value":"At least 6"},{"label":"Prisoners claimed in Grant’s May 11 dispatch","value":"5,000"},{"label":"New York Herald casualty estimate, May 18","value":"About 35,000","note":"Army of the Potomac, excluding Sheridan’s cavalry"},{"label":"Gold price, May 10","value":"$$168 in greenbacks per $100 gold"},{"label":"Gold price, May 18","value":"$$184 in greenbacks per $100 gold"},{"label":"Troops requested in the false proclamation","value":"400,000"}],"maps":[{"title":"Where the campaign was being fought","caption":"The Overland Campaign moved southeast from the Rapidan River through the Wilderness, Spotsylvania, and the North Anna. Reports from this corridor shaped public opinion in Washington and New York.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"1864 military map of Grant’s campaign routes across eastern Virginia"},{"title":"The campaign corridor today","caption":"The principal May battlefields lie between the Rapidan River and Richmond. Grant kept moving southeast after each battle instead of withdrawing north.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-78.15%2C37.45%2C-76.95%2C38.55&layer=mapnik&marker=38.2042%2C-77.5885"},{"title":"From the front to the national press","caption":"Military reports traveled from Virginia to the War Department in Washington. Stanton’s dispatches then went to the Associated Press and newspapers centered in New York.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-78.60%2C37.10%2C-73.20%2C41.25&layer=mapnik&marker=38.9072%2C-77.0369"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:21","audioTime":81,"title":"The episode asks what the casualties achieved","detail":"Soldiers and civilians evaluated the campaign while its outcome was still unknown. Their conclusions affected military morale, public support, and the 1864 election."},{"time":"2:46","audioTime":166,"title":"U.S. soldiers initially thought the campaign was succeeding","detail":"Many soldiers believed they had performed well in the Wilderness, remained in the field against Lee, and gained a clear success at the Mule Shoe."},{"time":"3:34","audioTime":214,"title":"The Mule Shoe shaped early opinion","detail":"U.S. forces broke part of Lee’s line, captured more than 3,000 prisoners and 20 cannon, and forced the Confederates to build a new position farther back."},{"time":"4:24","audioTime":264,"title":"Lincoln approved of Grant’s decision to continue","detail":"John Hay recorded Lincoln’s view that another commander probably would have withdrawn across the Rapidan after the Wilderness. Grant instead moved toward Spotsylvania."},{"time":"5:24","audioTime":324,"title":"Republican newspapers reported a major victory","detail":"The New York Times, Herald, and Tribune treated incomplete reports as evidence that Lee was retreating and that Confederate military power had been seriously damaged."},{"time":"6:23","audioTime":383,"title":"Democratic newspapers used a more cautious line","detail":"They could not ignore apparent U.S. gains, but they warned that reverses remained possible and criticized the Lincoln administration’s conduct of the war."},{"time":"7:35","audioTime":455,"title":"Newspapers relied on correspondents and official dispatches","detail":"Each major New York paper had several correspondents with the Army of the Potomac. Edwin Stanton also supplied reports based on Charles Dana’s observations and Grant’s dispatches."},{"time":"8:45","audioTime":525,"title":"Grant’s May 11 dispatch became a public slogan","detail":"Grant reported six days of heavy fighting, heavy losses on both sides, 5,000 Confederate prisoners, and his intention to continue the campaign through the summer if necessary."},{"time":"10:03","audioTime":603,"title":"The Mule Shoe produced another surge of confidence","detail":"After news of the May 12 attack arrived, both Republican and Democratic papers described it as a major U.S. success and a step toward Richmond."},{"time":"12:33","audioTime":753,"title":"Newspapers also reported the high casualty cost","detail":"Published estimates varied widely. By May 18, the New York Herald estimated about 35,000 Army of the Potomac casualties, excluding Sheridan’s cavalry."},{"time":"13:42","audioTime":822,"title":"Confidence declined when Lee remained in the field","detail":"Readers learned that the Confederate army had withdrawn to another strong line rather than collapsed. Reports of U.S. defeats at New Market and Drewry’s Bluff added to the change in opinion."},{"time":"15:56","audioTime":956,"title":"Northern opinion became more pessimistic after May 12","detail":"Soldiers, officials, and civilians noted that the campaign had produced heavy losses without destroying Lee’s army or ending the fighting."},{"time":"17:38","audioTime":1058,"title":"The gold market reflected changing expectations","detail":"The greenback price of $100 in gold fell from $179.50 on May 4 to $168 on May 10, then rose to $184 on May 18 as confidence in U.S. military success weakened."},{"time":"18:48","audioTime":1128,"title":"A false presidential proclamation disrupted the market","detail":"Joseph Howard Jr. supplied newspapers with a fabricated Lincoln proclamation that claimed Grant’s campaign was stopping and called for 400,000 additional troops."},{"time":"19:49","audioTime":1189,"title":"Two newspapers printed the false document","detail":"The New York Journal of Commerce and New York World published it before learning it was fraudulent. Both retracted it, but Stanton ordered their editors arrested."},{"time":"20:47","audioTime":1247,"title":"Other publishers demanded the editors’ release","detail":"Republican newspaper publishers joined the appeal. The editors were released on May 23, while Howard spent about three months at Fort Lafayette."},{"time":"21:37","audioTime":1297,"title":"Confederate public opinion remained confident","detail":"Many Confederates interpreted Grant’s continued movement as refusal to admit defeat, not evidence that Lee had lost control of the campaign."},{"time":"23:58","audioTime":1438,"title":"Southern newspapers explained Grant’s persistence as necessity","detail":"Editorials argued that Grant could not withdraw without political and military consequences, even though they claimed Lee had already defeated him."},{"time":"25:00","audioTime":1500,"title":"The image of Grant as a butcher began during the campaign","detail":"The Richmond Examiner portrayed Grant’s strategy as a deliberate exchange of U.S. casualties for proportionate Confederate losses."},{"time":"27:14","audioTime":1634,"title":"Lee was also accepting losses his army could not easily replace","detail":"The episode notes that Confederate forces had fewer soldiers available and that Lee continued spending those lives to keep the Confederacy in the war."},{"time":"27:46","audioTime":1666,"title":"The campaign’s value was not yet known","detail":"At the end of May, neither side knew the campaign’s final outcome. The casualties therefore could not yet be measured against a completed military result."}],"command":[{"side":"United States — command and information chain","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Abraham Lincoln","command":"President","subordinates":"Evaluated Grant’s performance and depended on continued public support for the war"},{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Directed the campaign and sent military dispatches to Washington"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Managed the principal field army operating against Lee"},{"commander":"Charles A. Dana","command":"Assistant secretary of war in the field","subordinates":"Reported observations from army headquarters to Edwin Stanton"},{"commander":"Edwin M. Stanton","command":"Secretary of War","subordinates":"Prepared public dispatches for John A. Dix and the Associated Press"},{"commander":"John A. Dix","command":"Department of the East","subordinates":"Nominal recipient of Stanton’s dispatches in New York"}]},{"side":"Confederacy — military command and public interpretation","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Jefferson Davis","command":"President","subordinates":"Led a government that depended on continued military resistance and civilian support"},{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Kept his army between Grant and Richmond through the May battles"},{"commander":"Richmond Examiner and other Southern papers","command":"Public reporting and editorials","subordinates":"Presented the Wilderness and Spotsylvania as Confederate successes and criticized Grant’s casualty cost"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"Praised Grant for continuing the campaign after the Wilderness and faced the political consequences of its casualties","side":"United States","image":"/images/abraham-lincoln-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008680391/"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Continued moving south after each battle and became the focus of competing claims about victory and casualties"},{"name":"Edwin M. Stanton","role":"Controlled official War Department reporting and ordered arrests after two newspapers printed the false proclamation","side":"United States","image":"/images/edwin-stanton.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666385/"},{"name":"John Hay","role":"Lincoln’s secretary who recorded the president’s comments about Grant","side":"United States"},{"name":"Charles A. Dana","role":"Assistant secretary of war who reported from the Army of the Potomac","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Howard Jr.","role":"Created the false May 18 proclamation to help gold speculators profit from market disruption","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Templeton Strong","role":"New York diarist who documented declining confidence in Grant’s campaign","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Kept the Army of Northern Virginia intact and blocked Grant’s route to Richmond during May","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical military map of Grant’s 1864 campaign routes in Virginia","caption":"The army’s movement through eastern Virginia continued while newspapers tried to determine whether each battle was a victory or defeat.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"image":"/images/abraham-lincoln-1864.jpg","alt":"Abraham Lincoln seated in January 1864","caption":"Lincoln believed Grant’s decision to remain on the offensive distinguished him from earlier Army of the Potomac commanders.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008680391/"},{"image":"/images/ulysses-s-grant.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Ulysses S. Grant","caption":"Grant’s dispatches and continued movement shaped public debate over the campaign’s results and cost.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"image":"/images/edwin-stanton.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Secretary of War Edwin Stanton","caption":"Stanton distributed official reports to the press and ordered the arrests connected to the false proclamation.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666385/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 497","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/07/13/civil-war-podcast-episode-497/"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Military Experience","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-military-experience.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: the 1864 election and war weariness","url":"https://www.nps.gov/pete/learn/historyculture/the-1864-election.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1864 Civil War timeline","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/civil-war-glass-negatives/articles-and-essays/time-line-of-the-civil-war/1864/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Grant’s 1864 Virginia campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Civil War in America exhibition","url":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/november-1863-april-1865.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: New York Herald newspaper record","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn83030313/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Edwin M. Stanton portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666385/"}]}}]}]
